{"conditionalSetIds":[],"forecasterId":"demographics-ai-2036:run2:growth:bayes","informationSetArtifactId":"sha256:fe5d2cb4981918600c26a5938d0456e58876b366f2e3317d8ff0e3b73f4b163b","modelForecastIds":[],"modelUse":"none","prediction":{"kind":"categorical","probabilities":{"A":0.11,"B":0.49,"C":0.26,"D":0.14}},"questionHash":"sha256:ca2a4311940635fba34c40e7278721deb2f4716732e6011c94e6eb0554263e61","questionId":"demographics-ai-2036-cq6","questionVersion":"1.0.0","rationale":"PRIOR ADOPTED: the Historian's 17 / 45 / 27 / 11. I then re-derive every cell from MY OWN posterior CQ3 and CQ2 under the lexical-priority lock, because the lock makes CQ6 a deterministic function of those two answers and any independently assigned cell would be an inconsistency rather than a forecast.\nMY CQ3 POSTERIOR: P10 0.75 / P50 1.75 / P90 2.65. Implied sigma: right side (2.65-1.75)/1.2816 = 0.702; left side (1.75-0.75)/1.2816 = 0.780 (left-skewed, as the AI-Q4 impairment prior and the discounted private-credit signal require).\nSTEP 1, A if coupled growth >= 2.6%. z = (2.6-1.75)/0.702 = 1.211, so P = 0.113. A = 11. I did NOT assign A independently; it is read off my own band. Cross-check against the locked 2B: standalone Transformative Breakout is 16.2%, and my coupled A at 11 must sit BELOW it, because A demands coupled real growth >= 2.6%/yr delivered against a -1.17pp/yr coupled aging drag (0.335 x -2.7 for China plus 0.665 x -0.4 for the OECD), not merely a TFP breakout. Endogenizing demographics makes A strictly harder than the exogenous-demographics breakout. Any panelist at or above 16.2% here has failed to couple.\nSTEP 2, else C if OECD-38 core >= 2.7% AND growth < 2.0%. P(growth < 2.0) = P(z < 0.356) = 0.639. Inflation leg, derived from MY CQ2 conjunct A of 0.72 at the >= 2.50% gate over 2027-36: raising the threshold to 2.70% and narrowing to the later 2032-36 window pulls down for mean reversion and up for fiscal dominance, giving P = 0.37. This is far above the Fermi 0.27 and