shold on a subwindow must be; C at 26 is therefore bounded by what my own CQ2 permits. (iv) D at 14 requires BOTH legs, and since my CQ2 arithmetic makes sub-2.0% OECD core the less likely OECD state (0.19) while making China deflation likely (0.62), D is correctly the smaller of the two low-growth cells despite my carrying a high impairment probability. (v) B at 49 absorbs the lock's two leftover cells — growth 2.0-2.6% with core >= 2.7%, and growth < 1.7% with core 2.0-2.7% — so the residual exceeding the standalone Jagged Diffusion mass of 47.2% is required by the lock's rule 4, not an error. My implied leftover is P(growth < 2.0) - C - D = 0.639 - 0.265 - 0.135 = 0.239, which sits inside B, leaving about 0.25 of B as the 2.0-2.6% growth band.\nTHE SIMULATION: the Fermi seat discloses CQ6 as PARTIAL — only the growth leg is modellable and the model cannot place mass between C and D because it has no price level. Its growth readings would put A at or above 50% on five of nine scenarios. LR about 1.1 after the four structural discounts published in my cq3_reasoning. REJECTED as a point input; worth nothing here.\nDIRECTION: MODERATED on A (17 -> 11, -6, forced by my narrower CQ3 P90 of 2.65 against the Historian's 2.9), AMPLIFIED on B (45 -> 49, +4) and on D (11 -> 14, +3), and essentially unmoved on C (27 -> 26). The net effect of my updating is to concentrate mass into the barbell residual and away from the productivity-renaissance tail.","referenceClassIds":[],"schemaVersion":"forecast-workbench.forecast/v1","sealedAt":"2026-11-04T17:31:59.517Z","seriesId":"published-panel","strongestContraryCase":"Not elicited per question. Panelist falsification signposts: {\"signpost\":\"OECD's own published GDP-weighted OECD-total core inflation aggregate (OECD Main Economic Indicators / CPI database) prints below 2.0% year-on-year for four consecutive quarters. This directly tests the composition argument (Turkey, Mexico, Colombia, Chile, Costa Rica holding the aggregate above the G7 core) that is the load-bearing non-AI assumption in my whole payload.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2029\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"My CQ2 falls from 40 to about 24 (conjunct A from 0.72 to roughly 0.45). CQ6 inverts in its low-growth half: C from 26 down to about 15, D from 14 up to about 24, because the <2.00% condition that currently starves D becomes live. In the asset matrix the regime probabilities shift mass from C to D, which flips G7 long nominal sovereigns from my worst conditional holding to one of my best. This is the single cheapest observation that falsifies me.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"FRED DFII10 (US 10y TIPS) 12-month moving average falls below 1.20% at any point, i.e. roughly 140bp below the locked 30 Oct 2026 print of 2.60%, without an accompanying MSCI World drawdown of >= 25% (so it is a level shift, not a flight-to-quality spike).\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2030\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"My CQ4 P50 falls from 2.05 to about 1.50 and P10 from 1.10 to about 0.55, restoring the Historian's Reinhart-Sbrancia repression tail that my 3:1 likelihood ratio on the TIPS anchor currently cuts. CQ5 falls from 22 to about 15 via the rate-level channel into the MOVE floor. In the asset matrix G7 long nominals and TIPS improve across all four regimes and my 2026-2030 buildout numbers for cash and ILBs are too high.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"Generator step-up transformer lead times fall back below 80 weeks, and gas turbine lead times below 36 months, on OEM order-book disclosures or NERC/EIA reporting (against >160 weeks and five years respectively as of Q1 2026).\",\"by_when\":\"Jun 30, 2030\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"The 2.5:1 likelihood ratio that does most of the downward work in my CQ3 collapses. CQ3 P50 rises from 1.75 to about 1.95 and P90 from 2.65 back to about 2.90; CQ6-A rises from 11 to about 16-17 and B falls correspondingly. It also raises realized AI-Q3 datacentre load back toward the locked 998 TWh P50 and cuts my upward correction to AI-Q4. Power/grid and construction conditional returns fall (the scarcity rent disappears), AI-complex conditional returns rise.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"China headline CPI (NBS monthly release) 12-month average exceeds 2.0% for four consecutive quarters, with core ex-food-and-energy above 1.8% — i.e. the PPI +5.0% signal of Sep-2026 transmits to the household price level rather than decoupling as it did in 2021.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2030\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"My CQ2 conjunct B (0.62) is wrong and CQ2 falls from 40 to about 22. It also means the Chinese demand deficit I have priced is resolving, which raises my China growth leg above 2.8%, lifts CQ3 P50 by roughly 0.10-0.15pp through the 0.335 PPP weight, and improves China equities across every regime while worsening onshore duration.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"Eurostat Structure of Earnings Survey or the OECD earnings database shows ISIC F+Q real median hourly wage growth leading J+K+M by >= 3.0 percentage points cumulative since 2025 (i.e. on pace for the +10pp gate), rather than the dead heat the Atlanta Fed tracker currently shows at 4.2 against 4.2.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2030\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"My CQ1 rises from 9 to about 25 (conjunct C2 from 0.15 to roughly 0.45), and the Stanford/ADP hiring-volume-not-pay mechanism that is my only above-2:1 signal on that question is falsified. It also means physical scarcity is clearing by PRICE rather than by rationing, which raises CQ6-C from 26 toward 32 through the cost-push channel and makes construction/trades and healthcare operators materially better holdings than I have priced in regimes B and C.\"}","updateBasis":{"kind":"launch","note":"run2 wave 2 bayes seat, effort 'growth'."}}